IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `tco-dates`
IMPORT `tco-rules`

-- Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) -- the questions of the FAMILY MEMBER (and other person with an interest) in a coroner's case.
--
-- SEAT: the individual the Act is applied to when someone has died (or a fire or explosion has happened) and a claim
-- may follow: the family member of the person who died -- above all the senior next of kin -- and any other person a
-- coroner considers has a sufficient interest (for example the family pursuing a workplace death, the owner of a
-- damaged property, the person whose belongings the coroner holds). "Am I the senior next of kin?", "Must there be an
-- inquest?", "By when can I go to the Supreme Court?", "Can the autopsy go ahead yet?", "What can I do at the
-- inquest?", "Can what was said at the inquest be used in my claim?", "Must I be given the records?", "Can the findings
-- be reopened?", "Can I get my property back?". The coroner, the Chief Magistrate, the Attorney-General, the police and
-- the Supreme Court get no seat: their judgments are asked as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, dates, hours and minutes, numbers, a choice from a list) and calls the
-- rules of tco-rules.l4. The time limits and bars are DEONTIC rules run inside the questions; no question takes a
-- DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (c) State of Tasmania, CC BY
-- 4.0; not the authorised version. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Coroners Act 1995 (Tas): the family's questions`

§§ `Who I am`

@export Am I the "senior next of kin" of the person who died (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 3A)? In order: the only spouse or significant-relationship partner (if there were several, the last; another one only with the last one's approval when the last is not available); then an adult child; then a partner in a registered caring relationship; then a parent; then an adult sibling; then the executor or personal representative -- each only if no one higher is available. If the deceased was Aboriginal, the person who is appropriate by the community's custom may be the senior next of kin. The senior next of kin is notified of decisions and can go to the Supreme Court; a decision naming the senior next of kin can be appealed to the Supreme Court (s 3B).
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `Relationship to the deceased` @desc Your relationship to the person who died: their spouse or significant-relationship partner, a child, the partner in a registered caring relationship (deed registered under the Relationships Act 2003), a parent, a sibling, the executor or personal representative, or none of these
      `am I 18 or over` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are 18 or older
      `was I the only spouse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a spouse or partner: whether you were the person's only spouse or partner immediately before the death
      `were there several spouses` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a spouse or partner: whether the person was survived by more than one spouse or partner
      `was I the last to qualify` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If there were several: whether you were the last of them to become the person's spouse or partner
      `is the last spouse available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If there were several and you were not the last: whether the last spouse or partner is available
      `does the last spouse approve me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If there were several and you were not the last: whether the last spouse or partner approves of you acting
      `is a spouse available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are not a spouse or partner: whether a spouse or partner who would rank first is available
      `is an adult child available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a child of the person aged 18 or over (other than you) is available
      `is the registered caring partner available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a partner in a registered caring relationship with the person (other than you) is available
      `is a parent available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a parent of the person (other than you) is available
      `is an adult sibling available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a sibling of the person aged 18 or over (other than you) is available
      `is another person of my kind available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone else in the same position as you (another adult child, parent, sibling, executor or eligible spouse) is also available
      `was the deceased Aboriginal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died was an Aboriginal person
      `am I the appropriate person by custom` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether, by the customs and tradition of their community or group, you are the appropriate person
GIVETH A `Senior next of kin answer`
`Am I the senior next of kin` MEANS
    `s 3A -- am I the senior next of kin` `my relationship` `am I 18 or over` `was I the only spouse` `were there several spouses` `was I the last to qualify` `is the last spouse available` `does the last spouse approve me` `is a spouse available` `is an adult child available` `is the registered caring partner available` `is a parent available` `is an adult sibling available` `is another person of my kind available` `was the deceased Aboriginal` `am I the appropriate person by custom`

@export Can I appeal to the Supreme Court against a decision about who is the senior next of kin (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 3B)? Yes, if a decision under the Act has named the senior next of kin and you are aggrieved by it. The Court may affirm it, or quash it and make further orders.
GIVEN `has a decision named the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a decision has been made under the Coroners Act specifying who is the senior next of kin
      `am I aggrieved by it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are aggrieved by that decision
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I appeal who is the senior next of kin` MEANS
    `s 3B -- I may appeal who is the senior next of kin` `has a decision named the senior next of kin` `am I aggrieved by it`

§§ `The death`

@export Is this a "reportable death" that must go to the coroner (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 3)? It is if the body is in Tasmania, or the death or its cause happened in Tasmania, or the person was travelling to or from Tasmania, and the death was unexpected, unnatural or violent or from an accident or injury; unexpected during or after a medical procedure; of unknown cause; a sudden unexpected infant death; of a person in care or custody; of an unidentified person; or at work and not apparently natural. It also is if a Tasmanian resident died elsewhere and no local doctor certified the cause, or the person died escaping custody or while being detained. A suspected death counts.
GIVEN `what kind of death` IS A `Kind of death` @desc Which description best fits the death (choose the expected-natural-causes option if none fits)
      `is the body in Tasmania` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body is in Tasmania
      `did the death occur in Tasmania` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred in Tasmania
      `did the cause occur in Tasmania` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the cause of the death (for example the accident or injury) occurred in Tasmania
      `was the person travelling to or from Tasmania` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died while travelling to or from Tasmania
      `did a Tasmanian resident die outside Tasmania` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person ordinarily lived in Tasmania and died somewhere outside Tasmania
      `was the cause certified there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether the cause of death was certified by a medical practitioner under the law of the place where they died
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it a reportable death` MEANS
    `s 3 -- it is a reportable death` `what kind of death` `is the body in Tasmania` `did the death occur in Tasmania` `did the cause occur in Tasmania` `was the person travelling to or from Tasmania` `did a Tasmanian resident die outside Tasmania` `was the cause certified there`

@export Must I report the death (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) ss 19, 20)? If you have reasonable grounds to believe that a reportable death has not been reported, you must report it as soon as possible to a coroner or a police officer (maximum fine 10 penalty units), and then give the coroner any information that may help. A death in custody or care, or while escaping or being detained, is reported by the custodian or officer instead. A doctor present need not report a death another doctor present has reported, but must give the coroner helpful information.
GIVEN `do I have reasonable grounds to believe it is reportable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reasonable grounds to believe the death is a reportable death (see the reportable death question)
      `do I have reasonable grounds to believe it is unreported` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reasonable grounds to believe it has not yet been reported to a coroner or the police
      `was it a custody, care, escape or detention death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was held in custody or care immediately before death, was escaping custody, or died while an officer was trying to detain them
      `am I the custodian or the officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether you are the person in whose custody or care they were, or the officer who was trying to detain them
      `am I a doctor present where another doctor reported it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a medical practitioner present at or after the death and another medical practitioner present has reported it
GIVETH A `Reporting answer`
`Must I report the death` MEANS
    `s 19 -- must I report the death` `do I have reasonable grounds to believe it is reportable` `do I have reasonable grounds to believe it is unreported` `was it a custody, care, escape or detention death` `am I the custodian or the officer` `am I a doctor present where another doctor reported it`

§§ `The inquest`

@export Can I ask the coroner to hold an inquest, to direct an autopsy, or to investigate a fire or explosion (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) ss 27(1), 37(1), 42(1))? Yes, if the coroner considers you have a sufficient interest (for an autopsy, the coroner must also have jurisdiction over the death). If the coroner refuses, you get written reasons and may go to the Supreme Court: within 14 days for an inquest, within 48 hours for an autopsy. If you ask for a fire investigation you must give the coroner any information that may help.
GIVEN `what do I ask for` IS A `Request to the coroner` @desc What you want to ask the coroner for: an inquest into a death, an autopsy, or an investigation into a fire or explosion
      `does the coroner consider I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner considers you have a sufficient interest in the death, fire or explosion (the coroner's judgment)
      `does the coroner have jurisdiction over the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has jurisdiction to investigate the death (it appears to the coroner that it is or may be a reportable death)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I ask the coroner` MEANS
    `ss 27, 37, 42 -- I may ask the coroner` `what do I ask for` `does the coroner consider I have a sufficient interest` `does the coroner have jurisdiction over the death`

@export Must there be an inquest into the death (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) ss 21, 23, 24, 24A, 26A)? A coroner must hold one where there is a Tasmanian connection and the coroner suspects homicide, the person was in care or custody, the identity is unknown, the person died escaping custody or being detained, another Act requires one, or the Attorney-General or Chief Magistrate directs. A death at work that the coroner is not satisfied was natural also requires one, but the senior next of kin may, within 14 days of notice, ask in writing for it not to be held, and the coroner may then decline if that is not against the public interest or the interests of justice. Otherwise an inquest may still be held if the coroner or Chief Magistrate thinks it desirable.
GIVEN `does the coroner consider the death may be reportable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the coroner that the death is or may be a reportable death (otherwise the coroner has no jurisdiction)
      `have the remains been reported to be Aboriginal remains` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an approved Aboriginal organisation has reported to the coroner that the remains are Aboriginal remains buried by Aboriginal custom
      `is there an investigation or inquest in another State or Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an investigation or inquest into the death is being held in another State or a Territory
      `has the Attorney-General directed an inquest or investigation here` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Attorney-General has directed an inquest (or that the coroner investigate despite the interstate one)
      `is there a Tasmanian connection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body is in Tasmania, or it appears to the coroner that the death or its cause happened in Tasmania, or the person ordinarily lived in Tasmania
      `does the coroner suspect homicide` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner suspects homicide
      `was the person in care or custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was held in care or in custody immediately before death
      `is the identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the identity of the person who died is not known
      `did the person die escaping custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died escaping or attempting to escape from prison, a detention centre, a secure mental health unit, police custody or court custody
      `did the person die while being detained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death occurred while a police, correctional or mental health officer or prescribed person was trying to detain a person
      `does another Act require an inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another Act requires an inquest because of where or how the death happened
      `has the Chief Magistrate directed an inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Chief Magistrate has directed an inquest
      `did the person die at work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died at their place of work, or from an accident or injury that happened there
      `is the coroner not satisfied it was natural` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is not satisfied that the death was due to natural causes
      `did the senior next of kin ask in time not to hold it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a workplace death: whether the senior next of kin asked the coroner in writing, within 14 days of notice of the decision to hold the inquest, not to hold it
      `is the coroner satisfied that is not against the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether the coroner is satisfied that not holding the inquest would not be contrary to the public interest or the interests of justice
GIVETH A `Inquest answer`
`Must there be an inquest` MEANS
    `ss 21-26A -- must there be an inquest` `does the coroner consider the death may be reportable` `have the remains been reported to be Aboriginal remains` `is there an investigation or inquest in another State or Territory` `has the Attorney-General directed an inquest or investigation here` `is there a Tasmanian connection` `does the coroner suspect homicide` `was the person in care or custody` `is the identity unknown` `did the person die escaping custody` `did the person die while being detained` `does another Act require an inquest` `has the Chief Magistrate directed an inquest` `did the person die at work` `is the coroner not satisfied it was natural` `did the senior next of kin ask in time not to hold it` `is the coroner satisfied that is not against the public interest`

@export Will the inquest be put off because someone has been charged over the death, fire or explosion (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 25)? If the coroner learns, before making a finding, that a person has been charged before justices with murder, manslaughter, infanticide or grievous bodily harm of the deceased, a dangerous-driving or Traffic Act s 32(1) offence from the fatal accident, arson or unlawfully causing the fire or explosion, the coroner must adjourn the inquest until the criminal proceedings end (including appeals that need no extension of time), unless there is reason not to. Afterwards the coroner may resume it as a fresh inquest if there is sufficient cause, and may make no finding inconsistent with the result of a trial on indictment.
GIVEN `what was the charge` IS A `Charge` @desc The offence the person was charged with
      `was the person charged before justices` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person has been charged before justices (a magistrate) with it
      `was the coroner told before making a finding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner was told of the charge before making a finding
      `is there reason to the contrary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is a reason not to adjourn (the coroner's judgment)
      `have the criminal proceedings concluded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the criminal proceedings have concluded, meaning no further appeal can be made without an extension of time
      `does the coroner think there is sufficient cause to resume` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If they have: whether the coroner is of the opinion that there is sufficient cause to resume the inquest
GIVETH A `Adjournment answer`
`Is the inquest adjourned for the criminal case` MEANS
    `s 25 -- is the inquest adjourned for the criminal case` `what was the charge` `was the person charged before justices` `was the coroner told before making a finding` `is there reason to the contrary` `have the criminal proceedings concluded` `does the coroner think there is sufficient cause to resume`

@export What can I do at the inquest (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 52)? If the coroner considers you have a sufficient interest, you may appear in person or be represented by an Australian legal practitioner (or by anyone else with the coroner's permission), call, examine and cross-examine witnesses, and make submissions; you may also make submissions at any stage of the investigation, and the coroner may give you the statements and affidavits it intends to consider.
GIVEN `does the coroner consider I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner considers you have a sufficient interest (the coroner's judgment)
      `how will I appear` IS A `Way of appearing` @desc How you will appear: in person, by an Australian legal practitioner, or by another person with or without the coroner's permission
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I take part in the inquest this way` MEANS
    `s 52(4) -- I may appear, examine witnesses and make submissions` `does the coroner consider I have a sufficient interest` `how will I appear`

@export Can I use this from the coroner's case as evidence in my own court case, for example a compensation or damages claim (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) ss 29(2), 46(2), 54, 64)? The coroner's record of an investigation is not evidence in any court of any fact stated in it. What a witness said giving evidence at the inquest cannot be used against that witness in any civil or criminal proceeding, except a prosecution for perjury in giving it. The coroner cannot be made to give evidence of what the coroner learned in the investigation.
GIVEN `what do I want to use` IS A `Evidence item` @desc What you want to use: the coroner's record of a death or a fire or explosion investigation, what a witness said at the inquest (against that witness, or against someone else), or evidence from the coroner personally
      `what kind of proceeding` IS A `Proceeding` @desc The proceeding you want to use it in: a civil proceeding (such as a damages or compensation claim), a criminal proceeding, or a prosecution of the witness for perjury in giving that evidence
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I use it in my court case` MEANS
    `ss 29, 46, 54, 64 -- it can be used in my court case` `what do I want to use` `what kind of proceeding`

§§ `Going to the Supreme Court in time`
-- Arises only after a decision you can challenge has been made ("Have you been told of a decision?").

@export What is the last day for my 14-day step (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) ss 26(2), 26A(2), 27(3), 44(2))? Within 14 days after you receive notice of the decision: the senior next of kin may apply to the Supreme Court for an inquest the coroner or Chief Magistrate decided not to hold, or ask the coroner in writing not to hold a workplace death inquest; a person whose request for an inquest into a death, fire or explosion was refused may apply to the Supreme Court for one. The Court orders an inquest if it is necessary or desirable in the interests of justice.
GIVEN `which step` IS A `Fourteen-day step` @desc The step you want to take
      `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin of the person who died (see that question)
      `did I make the request that was refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the person who asked the coroner for the inquest and was refused
      `day I received notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you received written notice of the decision
GIVETH A `Step window`
`What is the last day for my 14-day step` MEANS
    `the 14-day window for` `which step` `am I the senior next of kin` `did I make the request that was refused` `day I received notice`

@export Can I still take my 14-day step on this day (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) ss 26(2), 26A(2), 27(3), 44(2))? Only on a day from the day you received notice up to and including the 14th day after it.
GIVEN `which step` IS A `Fourteen-day step` @desc The step you want to take
      `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin of the person who died
      `did I make the request that was refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the person who asked the coroner for the inquest and was refused
      `day I received notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you received written notice of the decision
      `day I take the step` IS A DATE @desc The day you would file the application or give the coroner the written request
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I still take my 14-day step on this day` MEANS
    `the 14-day step` `which step` `am I the senior next of kin` `did I make the request that was refused` `day I received notice` `taken on` `day I take the step`

@export Can I still make my 48-hour application to the Supreme Court at this time (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) ss 37(3), 38(3), 39(4))? Within 48 hours after receiving notice: of the coroner's refusal of your request for an autopsy (asking the Court to order one); of the coroner's decision that an autopsy is necessary after you, as senior next of kin, asked for none (asking the Court to order none); or, as senior next of kin, of the Chief Magistrate's order to exhume the body (asking the Court to stop it).
GIVEN `which application` IS A `Forty-eight-hour step` @desc The application you want to make
      `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin of the person who died
      `did I make the request` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the person who asked the coroner for the autopsy (or, as senior next of kin, asked for no autopsy)
      `day I received notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the notice of the refusal, decision or order
      `hour I received notice` IS A NUMBER @desc The hour you received it, 0 to 23 (24-hour clock)
      `minute I received notice` IS A NUMBER @desc The minute you received it, 0 to 59
      `day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you would file the application
      `hour I apply` IS A NUMBER @desc The hour you would file it, 0 to 23
      `minute I apply` IS A NUMBER @desc The minute you would file it, 0 to 59
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I still make my 48-hour application` MEANS
    `the 48-hour application` `which application` `am I the senior next of kin` `did I make the request` `received at` (`the moment` `day I received notice` `at` `hour I received notice` `h` `minute I received notice`) `made at` (`the moment` `day I apply` `at` `hour I apply` `h` `minute I apply`)

§§ `The body`
-- Arises while the body is held for the coroner.

@export Is the autopsy still barred at this time (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 38)? If you, as senior next of kin, asked the coroner not to direct an autopsy and the coroner decided one is necessary, it must not be performed until 48 hours after you were given written notice of that decision, unless the coroner believes it must be done immediately; and not at all if the Supreme Court orders that it not be performed.
GIVEN `did I ask for no autopsy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, as senior next of kin, asked the coroner not to direct an autopsy
      `did the coroner decide it is necessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner decided that an autopsy is necessary despite your request
      `does the coroner believe it is needed immediately` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner believes the autopsy needs to be performed immediately
      `has the Supreme Court ordered no autopsy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has ordered that an autopsy not be performed
      `day I was given notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given written notice of the coroner's decision
      `hour I was given notice` IS A NUMBER @desc The hour you were given it, 0 to 23
      `minute I was given notice` IS A NUMBER @desc The minute you were given it, 0 to 59
      `day of the autopsy` IS A DATE @desc The day the autopsy would be performed
      `hour of the autopsy` IS A NUMBER @desc The hour it would be performed, 0 to 23
      `minute of the autopsy` IS A NUMBER @desc The minute it would be performed, 0 to 59
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the autopsy still barred` MEANS
    `s 38 -- the autopsy is barred` `did I ask for no autopsy` `did the coroner decide it is necessary` `does the coroner believe it is needed immediately` `has the Supreme Court ordered no autopsy` (`the moment` `day I was given notice` `at` `hour I was given notice` `h` `minute I was given notice`) (`the moment` `day of the autopsy` `at` `hour of the autopsy` `h` `minute of the autopsy`)

@export Is the exhumation of the body still barred at this time (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 39)? The senior next of kin must be given at least 48 hours' written notice before the body is exhumed (unless the Chief Magistrate is satisfied that is not possible); if the senior next of kin asks the Chief Magistrate not to exhume it, it must not be exhumed until 48 hours after the request; and not at all if the Supreme Court orders that it not be exhumed.
GIVEN `has notice been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether written notice of the exhumation has been given to you as senior next of kin
      `is the Chief Magistrate satisfied notice is not possible` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Chief Magistrate is satisfied it is not possible to give the notice
      `day notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day notice was given (any date if none)
      `hour notice was given` IS A NUMBER @desc The hour notice was given, 0 to 23
      `minute notice was given` IS A NUMBER @desc The minute notice was given, 0 to 59
      `did I ask not to exhume` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, as senior next of kin, asked the Chief Magistrate not to exhume the body
      `day of my request` IS A DATE @desc The day you made that request (any date if none)
      `hour of my request` IS A NUMBER @desc The hour you made it, 0 to 23
      `minute of my request` IS A NUMBER @desc The minute you made it, 0 to 59
      `has the Supreme Court ordered no exhumation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has ordered that the body not be exhumed
      `day of the exhumation` IS A DATE @desc The day the body would be exhumed
      `hour of the exhumation` IS A NUMBER @desc The hour it would be exhumed, 0 to 23
      `minute of the exhumation` IS A NUMBER @desc The minute it would be exhumed, 0 to 59
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the exhumation still barred` MEANS
    `s 39 -- the exhumation is barred` `has notice been given` `is the Chief Magistrate satisfied notice is not possible` (`the moment` `day notice was given` `at` `hour notice was given` `h` `minute notice was given`) `did I ask not to exhume` (`the moment` `day of my request` `at` `hour of my request` `h` `minute of my request`) `has the Supreme Court ordered no exhumation` (`the moment` `day of the exhumation` `at` `hour of the exhumation` `h` `minute of the exhumation`)

@export Is the coroner still barred from releasing the body for burial or cremation (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 32)? The coroner must issue the certificate authorising disposal as soon as reasonably possible, but not while an application to the Supreme Court for an inquest (s 27) or an autopsy (s 37) is undecided or the time to make one is still running -- except that the coroner may go ahead before the s 27 time ends if the person who asked for the inquest says they will not apply -- and never if the Supreme Court has made an order under s 27 or s 37.
GIVEN `has the Supreme Court made an order under section 27 or 37` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has ordered an inquest (s 27) or an autopsy (s 37)
      `is an application under section 27 or 37 undecided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an application to the Supreme Court for an inquest or an autopsy has been made and not yet disposed of
      `is the time for a section 27 application running` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the 14 days to apply for an inquest after a refused request are still running
      `has the requester said they will not apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who asked for the inquest has told the coroner they do not intend to apply to the Supreme Court
      `is the time for a section 37 application running` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the 48 hours to apply for an autopsy after a refused request are still running
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the release of the body still barred` MEANS
    `s 32 -- the disposal certificate is barred` `has the Supreme Court made an order under section 27 or 37` `is an application under section 27 or 37 undecided` `is the time for a section 27 application running` `has the requester said they will not apply` `is the time for a section 37 application running`

§§ `The records`

@export Must I be given a copy of the coronial record I asked for, and by when (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 58C)? The senior next of kin may ask the Chief Magistrate or the coroner in writing for a copy of a coronial record (a pathology or autopsy report, a record or photograph with evidentiary material or made under the Act, or a transcript or recording of evidence). A copy must be given within 28 days, or a later agreed period, unless the authority is satisfied on reasonable grounds that release is legally prevented, would unreasonably intrude on someone else's privacy against the public interest, would prejudice an investigation, law enforcement or a fair trial, or is against national or personal security -- in which case it may refuse or give a redacted copy. You may appeal a refusal, a failure, or a no-publication order.
GIVEN `am I the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin of the person who died
      `did I ask in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked in writing to the Chief Magistrate or the coroner with jurisdiction over the death
      `what kind of record` IS A `Kind of record` @desc What the record is
      `is it held by or obtainable by the Coronial Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Magistrates Court (Coronial Division) holds the record, or it can lawfully be provided to that court
      `what ground for refusal applies` IS A `Refusal ground` @desc The ground for refusal the Chief Magistrate or coroner is satisfied of, on reasonable grounds (none if none)
      `day of my request` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the request
      `was a later period agreed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the Chief Magistrate or coroner agreed a period longer than 28 days
      `agreed last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day of that agreed period (any date if none)
GIVETH A `Records answer`
`Must I be given a copy of the record` MEANS
    `s 58C -- must I be given a copy of the record` `am I the senior next of kin` `did I ask in writing` `what kind of record` `is it held by or obtainable by the Coronial Division` `what ground for refusal applies` `day of my request` `was a later period agreed` `agreed last day`

@export Has the coroner or Chief Magistrate failed to give me the copy in time, so that I can appeal (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 58C(4), (10)(b))? For a request the authority has not refused, the copy is owed within 28 days after the request or a later agreed period; once that time has passed without a copy, or the copy came late, you may appeal the failure (to the Supreme Court if the Chief Magistrate dealt with the request, otherwise to the Chief Magistrate).
GIVEN `day of my request` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the written request
      `was a later period agreed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a period longer than 28 days was agreed
      `agreed last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day of that agreed period (any date if none)
      `have I been given the copy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given the copy
      `day I was given it` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given it (any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (usually today)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Has the copy been withheld past the time` MEANS
    `s 58C(4), (10)(b) -- the authority has failed to provide the copy` `day of my request` `was a later period agreed` `agreed last day` `have I been given the copy` `day I was given it` `today`

@export Who hears my appeal about the coronial records I asked for (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 58C(10)-(12))? The Supreme Court if the Chief Magistrate dealt with your request; otherwise the Chief Magistrate, from whom you may appeal again to the Supreme Court.
GIVEN `did the Chief Magistrate deal with my request` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Chief Magistrate (rather than the coroner) determined your request
GIVETH A `Appeal forum`
`Who hears my records appeal` MEANS
    `s 58C(11) -- who hears my records appeal` `did the Chief Magistrate deal with my request`

§§ `After the findings`
-- Arises only once the coroner has made findings.

@export Can the investigation be reopened on my application (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 58)? You may apply to the Chief Magistrate if he or she considers you have a sufficient interest in the findings. The Chief Magistrate may reopen it, or have another coroner do so, if satisfied of fraud, a lack of thoroughness or an irregularity, mistakes in the record, new facts or evidence, findings unsupported by the evidence, or another compelling reason -- but not while a Supreme Court application to void the inquest findings (s 58A) is pending, nor on substantially the grounds the Court has rejected. You may appeal a refusal to the Supreme Court.
GIVEN `does the Chief Magistrate consider I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Chief Magistrate considers you have a sufficient interest in the findings
      `is a section 58A application pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an application to the Supreme Court to declare the inquest findings void is before the Court
      `was a section 58A application rejected on the same grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has rejected such an application and your reasons are the same or substantially the same as its grounds
      `what ground is the Chief Magistrate satisfied of` IS A `Ground for reopening` @desc The ground the Chief Magistrate is satisfied of (no such ground if none)
GIVETH A `Reopening answer`
`Can the investigation be reopened` MEANS
    `s 58 -- can the investigation be reopened on my application` `does the Chief Magistrate consider I have a sufficient interest` `is a section 58A application pending` `was a section 58A application rejected on the same grounds` `what ground is the Chief Magistrate satisfied of`

@export Can the Supreme Court declare the inquest findings void on my application (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 58A)? Anyone may apply, even after the Chief Magistrate refused to reopen, with any evidence or grounds. The Court may declare all or any of the findings void if satisfied of the same grounds as for reopening; it must then order the inquest reopened or a new one held, by the Chief Magistrate or another coroner.
GIVEN `was there an inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the findings were made at an inquest (not only an investigation without an inquest)
      `what ground is the Court satisfied of` IS A `Ground for reopening` @desc The ground the Supreme Court is satisfied of (no such ground if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Supreme Court void the inquest findings` MEANS
    `s 58A -- the Supreme Court may declare the inquest findings void` `was there an inquest` `what ground is the Court satisfied of`

§§ `My property`
-- Arises only where the coroner or police hold something you own or are entitled to.

@export What can I do about my property that the coroner holds for the investigation (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) ss 60-63)? If you claim ownership of, a lien over or a right to possess it: during the investigation you may apply to the coroner for orders about its care and control; at the end the coroner must make orders for its custody or disposition (unless it is likely to be needed for other legal proceedings); you are entitled to be heard; and you may ask the Supreme Court to review an order, or the coroner to vary or revoke it after a substantial change in circumstances.
GIVEN `do I claim ownership, a lien or a right to possess it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim ownership of, a lien over, or a present or future right to possess the item
      `is it in the coroner's legal custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the item is in the legal custody of a coroner for an investigation
      `what stage are we at` IS A `Stage of the investigation` @desc The stage the coroner's case has reached
      `is the coroner satisfied it is needed for other proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied the item will be, or is likely to be, needed for other legal proceedings
      `has there been a substantial change` IS A BOOLEAN @desc After an order: whether there has been a substantial change in the circumstances it deals with
GIVETH A `Property answer`
`What can I do about my property held by the coroner` MEANS
    `ss 60-63 -- what I can do about my property held by the coroner` `do I claim ownership, a lien or a right to possess it` `is it in the coroner's legal custody` `what stage are we at` `is the coroner satisfied it is needed for other proceedings` `has there been a substantial change`

@export What happens to my item that the police took in an emergency at the scene (Coroners Act 1995 (Tas) s 59A)? If a coroner was already investigating, or an investigation begins within 12 months, it passes into the coroner's custody. Until then the police may keep it; if no investigation begins within 12 months it must be returned to the person it was taken from (or the person entitled to it), or disposed of as the Commissioner of Police directs if it is illicit.
GIVEN `was a coroner investigating when it was taken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner was already investigating the death, fire or explosion when the police took the item
      `day it was taken` IS A DATE @desc The day the police took the item
      `has an investigation commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner's investigation has since commenced
      `day the investigation commenced` IS A DATE @desc The day it commenced (any date if none)
      `is the item illicit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the item is illicit (unlawful to possess)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (usually today)
GIVETH A `Seized item answer`
`What happens to my item the police took` MEANS
    `s 59A -- what happens to the item the police took` `was a coroner investigating when it was taken` `day it was taken` `has an investigation commenced` `day the investigation commenced` `is the item illicit` `today`

§§ `Offences`

@export What is the most I can be fined for an offence under the Coroners Act 1995 (Tas), in penalty units? Not reporting a death, or not giving information: 10; entering a restricted scene or disobeying a summons: 10; obstructing a coroner: 20; publishing contrary to a coroner's order, or insulting a coroner or disrupting an inquest: 50. The dollar value of a penalty unit is set by the Penalty Units and Other Penalties Act 1987.
GIVEN `which offence` IS A `Offence` @desc The offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most I can be fined in penalty units` MEANS
    `the most penalty units for` `which offence`

@export What is the longest I can be imprisoned for an offence under the Coroners Act 1995 (Tas), in months (0 where the offence carries only a fine)? Entering a restricted scene or disobeying a coroner's summons, order or direction: 3 months; obstructing a coroner, or insulting a coroner or disrupting an inquest: 6 months.
GIVEN `which offence` IS A `Offence` @desc The offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the longest I can be imprisoned in months` MEANS
    `the most months of imprisonment for` `which offence`
